; TEST: expect-exit=50
;
; A narrow result from ANOTHER OBJECT is normalised before it is stored
; (emit.inc's `__bfa_emit_call`). SysV AMD64 leaves the bits of `rax` above
; a u8/u16/u32 return value unspecified, and every instruction after the
; call assumes the canonical form (ssa-ir.md 2.2), so a call to an
; `externus` callee whose return type is narrow gets the normalisation pair
; after the `call`. A callee this emitter wrote needs none: its `ret` loads
; a canonical slot.
;
; No prelude routine returns a narrow value today, so this fixture makes one
; out of a wide one: it declares the prelude's `exsrt_mundus_ambitus` --
; which returns a POINTER, an address with bits far above bit 7 -- as
; `-> u8`. That is exactly a foreign callee whose upper bits are garbage.
; After the normalisation the result is below 256; `zext u64` of a
; canonical u8 is a plain copy, so without it the slot would still hold the
; address and the compare would fail.
;
; Mutation (run): the normalisation after an externus call dropped -> exit 1.

functio @exsrt_mundus_ambitus (ptr) -> u8 numeri ad_parem vetita explicita conservata externus sysv_amd64 {
}
functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 numeri ad_parem vetita explicita conservata {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = call u8 @exsrt_mundus_ambitus %0
%2 = zext u64 %1
%3 = iconst u64 256
%4 = cmp.lt u64 %2 %3
br %4 b1 b2
b1:
%5 = iconst u8 50
ret %5
b2:
%6 = iconst u8 1
ret %6
}
